  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nMorning RAOB and GOES vapor imagery depicts an amplified upper \nlevel pattern, characterized by a long-wave trough over the \nwestern CONUS and an upper level ridge extending from TX into \nthe upper Midwest and Great Lakes region. These features are \nreflected at the surface as a lee-trough across eastern WY/CO, \nand an expansive area of high pressure centered over southern \nIN/OH. Locally, modest south-southwest winds and dry/sunny \nconditions were noted within the western periphery of the \nsurface high and beneath the upper level ridge axis.\n\nCurrent vapor imagery shows a vigorous mid-level short wave \nrounding the base of the upper trough across NV/CA/AZ. This wave\nis progged to lift northeast across the Rockies tonight and the\nNorthern Plains on Wednesday, with an associated surface low. \nFor the western Great Lakes region the main impacts will be \nincreasingly breezy south-southwest surface winds and \npersistent low-level warm advection. While strong synoptic-scale\nforcing will spread across the upper Midwest, the current \nsurface ridge axis from TX to the mid-Atlantic region will \nprovide an obstacle to low-mid level moisture advection from the\nwestern Gulf. Thus, dry weather is expected to persist across \nour forecast area with mainly just patchy high-level cirrus \nspilling into the region at times tonight and Wednesday. As \nmentioned above, south-southwest surface winds will become \nbreezy Wednesday, with gusts 25-30 mph likely by afternoon, \nespecially across our northern IL cwa counties. Well-above \naverage temperatures are forecast, in the upper 50s/low 60s \nWednesday and in the low to mid 60s Thursday, as the upper \nMidwest surface low passes well to our north and the trailing \ncold front stalls north/northwest of the forecast area.\n\nAnother vigorous mid-level disturbance/upper low, currently \nevident in vapor imagery off the west coast, is progged to move \nashore and redevelop the long-wave trough over the western CONUS\nlate in the week. Latest ensemble guidance indicates at least a\ncouple of distinct short waves tracking across the Plains and \nupper Midwest from late Friday through Sunday night as the upper\ntrough eventually opens up and propagates to the east. \nInitially, guidance is in good agreement in the first of these \nwaves lifting a surface low across the upper Midwest \nFriday/Friday night, with dry and unseasonably warm conditions \ncontinuing (60s to potentially near 70). Meanwhile, the surface \nhigh/ridge over the southeastern CONUS gradually shifts \neastward, eventually allowing some return low-mid level moisture\nfrom the western Gulf to spread north into the Midwest over the\nweekend. Models have trended slightly slower with the next mid-\nlevel vort and surface wave, though the general consensus \ncontinues to indicate our next chance of rain may develop as \nearly as Saturday with some scattered showers in the warm \nadvection regime ahead of the approaching wave, and more likely \nlater Saturday night into Sunday morning with the actual cold \nfront. After another unseasonably warm day ahead of the \napproaching cold front Saturday with highs again in the 60s to \nnear 70, a trend toward much-cooler but seasonably average \nweather is expected into early next week.\n\nRatzer",
